# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What to set in Vercel's environment variables. A template — no real values.
#
# Committed on purpose: it holds no secrets, and the *shape* of this
# configuration is the part that is easy to get wrong and hard to diagnose.
#
# Set these in the Vercel dashboard (Project → Settings → Environment
# Variables), not in a file. Do not commit a filled-in copy.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THREE URLS, TWO ENDPOINTS. Both differ from what works on a laptop, for one
# measured reason each:
#
#   1. The direct endpoint `db.<ref>.supabase.co` is IPv6-ONLY. It has no A
#      record — only AAAA. It works locally because a laptop has IPv6; Vercel's
#      functions are IPv4, so a connection there fails to resolve. Every URL below
#      therefore uses the *pooler* hostname, which does publish an A record.
#
#   2. Port 6543 is the TRANSACTION pooler and 5432 on the same host is the
#      SESSION pooler. The distinction is not cosmetic: transaction mode hands
#      each statement to whichever backend is free, so a LISTEN is accepted and
#      then never delivered. Measured against this project's database — a NOTIFY
#      sent on 6543 never arrived, while the same test on 5432 delivered it
#      across two distinct backends. pg-boss and the realtime stream both depend
#      on that, which is why they get 5432.
#
# Pooled, transaction mode — Prisma queries from the web app. `pgbouncer=true`
# stops Prisma using prepared statements, which transaction mode cannot keep;
# `connection_limit=1` keeps each concurrent function to one connection.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.[PROJECT-REF]:[PASSWORD]@aws-0-[REGION].pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1&sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=[CERT-PATH]"

# Session mode — Prisma Migrate, and the realtime LISTEN held by the web process.
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.[PROJECT-REF]:[PASSWORD]@aws-0-[REGION].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=[CERT-PATH]"

# Session mode — the always-on worker (pg-boss). Note the worker does not run on
# Vercel: serverless functions cannot hold a queue open. It needs a host that
# runs a process continuously, and this variable belongs in that host's
# environment rather than here.
WORKER_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.[PROJECT-REF]:[PASSWORD]@aws-0-[REGION].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=[CERT-PATH]"

# ── [CERT-PATH] — the one value that needs checking on the first deploy ──────
#
# `certs/supabase-ca-2021.crt` is committed to this repository, and
# `next.config.ts` forces it into the deployed bundle via
# `outputFileTracingIncludes` (a certificate named inside a connection string is
# invisible to the file tracer, so it would otherwise be absent).
#
# What is NOT certain is the working directory of a Vercel function, which
# depends on the project's Root Directory setting. Try, in order:
#
#     ./certs/supabase-ca-2021.crt          (root directory = repo root)
#     ../../certs/supabase-ca-2021.crt      (root directory = apps/web)
#
# Verify on a PREVIEW deployment before production. A wrong path fails at
# runtime with ENOENT on the CA file — loud, not silent, which is the good case.
#
# If neither can be made to work, `sslmode=require` connects without verifying
# the certificate chain. That is encrypted but unauthenticated, and Supabase does
# not use a public CA, so it is a real downgrade rather than a formality. Prefer
# fixing the path.

# ── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate with `openssl rand -base64 48`. Never reuse the local development
# value. Rotating it invalidates every session, which is the point.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET="[48-BYTES-BASE64]"
BETTER_AUTH_URL="https://[YOUR-DOMAIN]"
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="https://[YOUR-DOMAIN]"

# ── CRM ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `mock` logs instead of pushing. Set `salesforce` only when the org is the one
# you want real enquiries landing in.
CRM_PROVIDER="mock"
# CRM_PROVIDER="salesforce"
# SALESFORCE_INSTANCE_URL="https://[YOUR-ORG].my.salesforce.com"
# SALESFORCE_CLIENT_ID="[CONNECTED-APP-CONSUMER-KEY]"
# SALESFORCE_CLIENT_SECRET="[CONNECTED-APP-CONSUMER-SECRET]"

# ── Behaviour ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV="production"
LOG_LEVEL="info"
INTAKE_MODE="lead_capture"
# Still `exclusive`. Not to be changed as part of a deployment.
DISPATCH_MODE="exclusive"
REALTIME_PROVIDER="postgres"
PAYMENT_PROVIDER="mock"
PAYOUT_PROVIDER="mock"
VIDEO_PROVIDER="mock"
MAILER_PROVIDER="mock"
MAIL_FROM="noreply@[YOUR-DOMAIN]"

# ── Not yet resolved, and deliberately absent ────────────────────────────────
#
# These are the pre-deployment gates in ARCHITECTURE.md §9a. Leaving them unset
# is honest; setting them to something plausible would hide the fact that they
# are not done:
#
#   - a real mailer (MAILER_PROVIDER/RESEND_API_KEY) — expert signup is gated on
#     email verification, so nobody can register an expert account without it
#   - a shared rate-limit store — the limiter is per-process, so N instances
#     means N times the limit
#   - object storage (STORAGE_*) — expert photos are on local disk today
#   - SENTRY_DSN — no error reporting
#   - a Content-Security-Policy
